What does the following log? <pre>for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  setTimeout(() => console.log(i), 0);
}</pre>

What does this log? <pre>function counter() {
  let count = 0;
  return () => ++count;
}
const c = counter();
console.log(c(), c(), c());</pre>

What does this log? <pre>function makeAdders() {
  const adders = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
    adders.push(x => x + i);
  }
  return adders;
}
const [add0, add1, add2] = makeAdders();
console.log(add0(10), add1(10), add2(10));</pre>

What does this log? <pre>const obj = {
  value: 42,
  getValue: function () {
    return (() => this.value)();
  },
};
console.log(obj.getValue());</pre>

Why does <code>this.seconds++</code> fail to update the class instance here? <pre>class Timer {
  seconds = 0;
  start() {
    setInterval(function () {
      this.seconds++;
    }, 1000);
  }
}</pre>

What is logged? <pre>function Animal(name) { this.name = name; }
Animal.prototype.speak = function () { return `${this.name} makes a noise.`; };
const a = new Animal('Rex');
delete a.speak;
console.log(a.speak());</pre>

What is logged, and in what order? <pre>console.log('A');
Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log('B'));
console.log('C');</pre>

What is the order of logged output? <pre>console.log('start');
setTimeout(() => console.log('timeout'), 0);
Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log('promise'));
console.log('end');</pre>

What is logged? <pre>const obj = { a: 1, nested: { b: 2 } };
const frozen = Object.freeze(obj);
frozen.nested.b = 99;
console.log(frozen.nested.b);</pre>

What does the rest parameter syntax do here? <pre>function sum(...nums) {
  return nums.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0);
}</pre>

What does this generic function signature describe? <pre>function wrap<T, K extends keyof T>(obj: T, key: K): T[K] {
  return obj[key];
}</pre>

What will TypeScript infer as the return type when this is called as <code>pair<string, number>('a', 1)</code>? <pre>function pair<A, B>(a: A, b: B): [A, B] {
  return [a, b];
}</pre>

What happens with the following? <pre>type A = { x: number };
type B = { y: string };
const value: A & B = { x: 1, y: 'hi' };</pre>

What does the <code>typeof</code> check let TypeScript do inside the if-block here? <pre>function format(value: string | number) {
  if (typeof value === 'string') {
    return value.toUpperCase();
  }
  return value.toFixed(2);
}</pre>

What TypeScript feature narrows <code>pet</code> to the <code>Fish</code> type here? <pre>type Fish = { swim: () => void };
type Bird = { fly: () => void };
function move(pet: Fish | Bird) {
  if ('swim' in pet) {
    pet.swim();
  }
}</pre>

What pattern is used to narrow the union safely here? <pre>type Shape =
  | { kind: 'circle'; radius: number }
  | { kind: 'square'; side: number };

function area(s: Shape) {
  if (s.kind === 'circle') return Math.PI * s.radius ** 2;
  return s.side ** 2;
}</pre>

What does this user-defined type guard enable? <pre>function isString(value: unknown): value is string {
  return typeof value === 'string';
}</pre>

What is TypeScript's shorthand for declaring and assigning a class property directly in the constructor's parameter list, as shown here? <pre>class Point {
  constructor(private x: number, private y: number) {}
}</pre>

What is the purpose of these function overload signatures? <pre>function reverse(value: string): string;
function reverse<T>(value: T[]): T[];
function reverse(value: any): any {
  // implementation
}</pre>

